1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,050
This video records from a real HTTP server, then stops it.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,950
doeff is a library for algebraic effects in Python.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:14,800
We follow send, a handler that sends with urllib.

4
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:19,750
Record once against the real service, and keep the file.

5
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:24,450
Then tests run from that file, with no network at all.

6
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,550
fetch opens the URL and waits for the response.

7
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:34,450
It returns the body as text. It blocks while it waits.

8
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:39,600
send builds a urllib Request from the effect.

9
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:45,200
It yields Await, so fetch runs on a worker thread.

10
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,650
With the body back, it resumes its k.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,200
The script starts a local server that answers like GitHub.

12
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:59,350
recorded with send: two requests go over HTTP.

13
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:03,300
The server answers twice, and the file keeps both.

14
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:06,550
Then the server stops.

15
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:11,500
The same program runs with offline, and the file answers.

16
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:15,200
latest_releases has no async anywhere.

17
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:21,300
VCR.py patches HTTP clients. A mock replaces a name.

18
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:25,950
This recorder sees only effects, whoever sends them.

19
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:30,250
So it can keep a model's answers or a random draw too.

20
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,200
Three files, doeff from main, and nothing else.

21
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:39,800
Record once, then replay in CI with offline.
